      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see the following comment in `__irq_msi_compose_msg()` in arch/x86/kernel/apic/apic.c:\n\n```\n\t/*\n\t * Only the IOMMU itself can use the trick of putting destination\n\t * APIC ID into the high bits of the address. Anything else would\n\t * just be writing to memory if it tried that, [...]\n\t */\n```\n\nSo it sounds like when the IOMMU generates an MSI with an address 0x100_FEE00000, it does not actually write at address 0x100_FEE00000, it writes at 0xFEE00000? And the address 0x100_FEE00000 itself is _not_ an APIC MMIO address, it is just an unrelated memory address which is not really involved here?\n\nSo this validation is unneeded, and moreover, it will make non-zero FEUADDR non-workable on machines with enough RAM (where 0x100_FEE00000 is an actual RAM address), i.e. pKVM will always refuse to set APIC IDs \u003e255 on those machines?",
      "revId": "acf672b261ba2b17d2fb843adec54ea9e8279cb8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "83ee2222_0c32a154",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/pkvm/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 36,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-28T13:39:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e So it sounds like when the IOMMU generates an MSI with an address 0x100_FEE00000, it does not actually write at address 0x100_FEE00000, it writes at 0xFEE00000? And the address 0x100_FEE00000 itself is not an APIC MMIO address, it is just an unrelated memory address which is not really involved here?\n\nI interpreted it a bit different. Based on my research, for 32bit APIC ID, normal device MSIs(\"Anything else\" in the comment) has to be programmed via IR tables. But IOMMU can directly access the MSI with FEUADDR:FEADDR (\"Only IOMMU itself can use the trick...\"). This is to avoid the chicken and egg problem where IR hardware needs to be initialized for interrupt remapping, and IR hardware cannot use it before fully initializing\". BIOS hopefully marks these regions as reserved if it indeed points to APIC address space. Thats the reason I used is_memory_range() to make sure that it is not a valid memory range. Unfortunately none of the official documents are clear about this and these details I gathered was from google and AI searches mostly. I thought to play safe and check it as implemented here.\n\n\u003e So this validation is unneeded, and moreover, it will make non-zero FEUADDR non-workable on machines with enough RAM (where 0x100_FEE00000 is an actual RAM address), i.e. pKVM will always refuse to set APIC IDs \u003e255 on those machines?\n\nIf BIOS sets it as reserved, is_memory_range() would make sure that we don\u0027t block 32bit APIC IDs right? If BIOS does not mark it correctly, a better check would be to make sure that the FEUADDR is in the range [0x0, 0xFFF0].\n\nThis is just my understanding based on my research. But I can remove the check and do only FEADDR validation if we can be sure that IOMMU doesn\u0027t do a memory write",
      "parentUuid": "353355d7_17d36c57",
      "revId": "acf672b261ba2b17d2fb843adec54ea9e8279cb8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "eaee8bc2_213d879d",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/pkvm/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 36,
