      "writtenOn": "2026-04-28T16:36:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e BIOS hopefully marks these regions as reserved if it indeed points to APIC address space.\n\nIf it indeed points to APIC address space, \"anything else\" would not \"just be writing to memory\" (as that comment in __irq_msi_compose_msg() says), it would be writing to that APIC address space?\n\nSo my interpretation is: it is _not_ APIC address space, it is just a normal memory address (in particular, it may well be used as RAM by software independently of MSIs if there is enough RAM), it\u0027s just that the SoC hardware (the PCIe root complex or whatever) knows that if an MSI is generated by the IOMMU, those upper bits in its address are not address bits but the higher bits of the APIC ID.\n\nTo confirm this, we\u0027d need to check it (e.g. via /proc/iomem) on an actual machine with \u003e\u003d2TB of RAM... \n\n@chuanxiao.dong@intel.corp-partner.google.com do you or Kevin happen to know which interpretation (Vineeth\u0027s or mine) is correct, or maybe you can ask someone at Intel to clarify that?\n\n\u003e If BIOS does not mark it correctly, a better check would be to make sure that the FEUADDR is in the range [0x0, 0xFFF0].\n\nI\u0027m not sure what you mean. Where does this range [0x0, 0xFFF0] come from? The FEUADDR holds the higher 24 bits of the APIC ID (in the FEUADDR\u0027s higher 24 bits), so any FEUADDR value in the range [0x100, 0xFFFFFF00] is valid, right?",
      "parentUuid": "83ee2222_0c32a154",
      "revId": "acf672b261ba2b17d2fb843adec54ea9e8279cb8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "99a12993_d4ce0255",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/pkvm/iommu.c",
